## 🛠️ Tools & Technologies

| Tool | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Lovable | Build the application interface and user experience |
| Supabase | Store project requests, generated plans, and processing statuses |
| PostgreSQL | Manage relational database records |
| n8n Cloud | Orchestrate automation workflows |
| AI Language Model | Analyze project information and generate structured plans |
| Webhooks | Trigger automation through HTTP requests |
| HTTP / REST APIs | Enable communication between application components |
| JSON | Exchange structured data between services |
| GitHub | Document the project and its architecture |

---

## 🔑 Key Design Decisions

### Decision 1 — Separate the Application from the Automation

**Decision:**

Use Lovable for the application interface and n8n Cloud for automation.

**Why:**

Separating frontend responsibilities from workflow execution improves maintainability and makes the system easier to extend, troubleshoot, and modify.

### Decision 2 — Use Supabase as the Central Data Store

**Decision:**

Store user requests and generated plans in separate, related database tables.

**Why:**

This supports structured data management, maintains relationships between requests and results, and enables the application to retrieve actual saved records.

### Decision 3 — Use Secure Server-Side Integration

**Decision:**

Trigger n8n through a protected server-side function rather than exposing private integration credentials in the browser.

**Why:**

This reduces the risk of exposing Webhook credentials and privileged database keys.

### Decision 4 — Separate AI Generation from Completion Status

**Decision:**

Use explicit processing statuses instead of assuming that accepting a Webhook request means plan generation has succeeded.

**Why:**

AI processing can fail, time out, or require additional processing time.

The application should report completion only after the real result has been saved successfully.

### Decision 5 — Store Structured AI Output

**Decision:**

Store generated phases, recommended actions, and risks in structured JSON-compatible fields.

**Why:**

Structured results are easier to validate, store, retrieve, and display consistently.

